// §5.10.3.1 on cross(): "The tolerances (time_tol and expr_tol) are specified
// as analog_expression and shall be non-negative. If a value of zero (0.0) is
// specified, the simulator shall apply a suitable value."
//
// A tolerance is a window the kernel narrows the crossing time into; a negative
// one names an empty window, so the argument is not merely unhelpful but
// unsatisfiable, and the clause's own fallback is written for zero and not for
// a sign error. Note the operand type: an analog_expression, not the
// constant_expression the 2014 text required, so "shall be non-negative" is not
// in general statically decidable. -1n is a literal precisely so that it is
// decidable here and the sign is the only defect — event_cross.va and
// event_cross_falling.va pass +1n through the same argument position and must
// keep compiling. A tolerance whose sign is only known at runtime is a
// different fixture and does not belong in a `//! reject`.
//
// VerA refuses it with E0517, the §5.10.3 event-argument code.
//
//! lrm 5.10.3.1
//! reject E0517
module ch5_cross_tolerance_negative(p);
  inout p; electrical p; real x;
  analog begin
    x = 0.0;
    @(cross(V(p) - 1.0, +1, -1n)) x = 1.0;
    I(p) <+ x;
  end
endmodule
